1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:10,702
Every litre of fuel and every cubic metre of gas travels a long road, from rock deep underground to the final consumer.

2
00:00:11,202 --> 00:00:21,395
That road has three parts. Upstream finds and produces. Midstream gathers, processes and transports. Downstream refines and delivers.

3
00:00:23,095 --> 00:00:29,351
Part one: upstream. Find the hydrocarbons, prove them, and bring them to the surface.

4
00:00:31,251 --> 00:00:40,015
It starts with organic material buried in sediment. Over geological time, heat and burial turn it into hydrocarbons.

5
00:00:40,515 --> 00:00:48,595
They migrate upward into porous reservoir rock, and collect where a trap and a sealing layer stop them from escaping.

6
00:00:49,095 --> 00:00:58,133
A reservoir is not an underground cave of oil. It is rock, with oil, gas and water held in tiny connected pores.

7
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:09,499
Geoscientists look for places where five things come together: a source, a migration path, a reservoir, a trap and a seal.

8
00:01:09,999 --> 00:01:23,751
They combine regional geology, old wells and geophysical surveys. The result is a prospect: an interpretation, not proof, with a chance of success and a range of possible volumes.

9
00:01:25,651 --> 00:01:34,305
A controlled source sends elastic waves into the ground: vibrating trucks on land, towed acoustic sources at sea.

10
00:01:34,805 --> 00:01:42,845
Receivers record the echoes from rock boundaries, and processing turns those recordings into an image of the structure.

11
00:01:43,345 --> 00:01:52,909
Interpreters map horizons, faults and possible traps. But a reflection is not a photograph of oil. Only a well can confirm it.

12
00:01:54,809 --> 00:02:03,492
An exploration well tests the idea. A drilling rig creates the well. It is not the pumpjack that may produce from it later.

13
00:02:03,992 --> 00:02:13,484
The drillstring turns a bit that breaks the rock. Drilling fluid is pumped down inside the pipe, and returns up the outside carrying the cuttings.

14
00:02:13,984 --> 00:02:21,270
As the well deepens, steel casing is run and cemented in stages, each one narrower than the last.

15
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:31,301
Blowout preventers at the wellhead are part of well control. Offshore, the rig may be a jack-up, a semisubmersible or a drillship.

16
00:02:33,201 --> 00:02:40,450
Logging tools, rock cores, pressure measurements and fluid samples reveal what the well has found.

17
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:49,789
Finding hydrocarbons is not enough. The field must flow well, hold enough recoverable volume, and have a route to market.

18
00:02:50,289 --> 00:03:01,244
Appraisal wells narrow the uncertainty. Then engineers choose a development plan: a tie-back to existing facilities, or a standalone development.

19
00:03:03,144 --> 00:03:14,426
Completion turns a drilled hole into a producing well: production tubing, packers, a wellhead tree, and perforations through casing and cement into the rock.

20
00:03:14,926 --> 00:03:28,247
Some wells are also stimulated. Acid can clean or improve the rock near the well. Hydraulic fracturing opens fractures, held open by proppant. These are options, not standard steps.

21
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:39,464
Fluids flow into the well because reservoir pressure is higher than the pressure in the wellbore. A choke at the surface regulates the flow.

22
00:03:39,964 --> 00:03:48,492
Over time, pressure falls, water cut rises and production declines. Reservoir management adapts the strategy.

23
00:03:48,992 --> 00:03:56,809
Artificial lift adds energy to the well: a rod pump, an electrical submersible pump, or gas lift.

24
00:03:57,309 --> 00:04:07,733
Pressure support is a different job. Water or gas is injected through separate wells to maintain pressure and sweep oil toward the producers.

25
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:22,156
Each well sends a mixed stream of oil, gas and water through a flowline to a manifold. Offshore, subsea wells connect to a platform or a floating unit through flowlines and risers.

26
00:04:22,656 --> 00:04:35,004
Along the way, pressure and temperature drop. That can cause wax, hydrates or liquid slugs, so operators use insulation, heating, chemicals and pigging to keep the flow moving.

27
00:04:36,904 --> 00:04:47,189
At the production facility, a separator slows the stream down. Gas rises to the top, water settles to the bottom, and oil floats between them.

28
00:04:47,689 --> 00:04:57,891
Level controls regulate each outlet. Several pressure stages recover more liquid, and the gas from every stage is collected and compressed.

29
00:04:59,791 --> 00:05:10,943
The oil is then treated. Heat, chemicals and electrostatic coalescers break the remaining emulsion, and stabilisation removes the lightest components.

30
00:05:11,443 --> 00:05:21,575
Produced water has its own treatment train, before reuse, reinjection or permitted disposal. The recovered oil goes back into the process.

31
00:05:23,475 --> 00:05:34,789
Meters and laboratory samples establish quantity and quality. Where streams from many wells are mixed, back allocation assigns the totals to individual wells.

32
00:05:35,289 --> 00:05:46,326
At the transfer point, a custody-transfer meter and a quality certificate record exactly what was delivered. Ownership changes. The fluid does not.

33
00:05:48,026 --> 00:05:55,342
Part two: midstream. Move the oil and the gas, process them, and store them on the way to market.

34
00:05:57,242 --> 00:06:06,001
Crude oil travels by pipeline, tanker, rail or truck. On a pipeline, pump stations restore the pressure lost to friction.

35
00:06:06,501 --> 00:06:18,106
Terminals store, measure and dispatch the crude. Tanks buffer the gap between production, shipping and refinery demand, and different grades travel in separate batches.

36
00:06:18,606 --> 00:06:30,330
Control rooms watch pressures and flows around the clock. Coatings and cathodic protection fight corrosion, and inspection pigs travel inside the line to check its condition.

37
00:06:32,230 --> 00:06:42,760
Now the gas. Raw gas carries water vapour, carbon dioxide, hydrogen sulfide and heavier hydrocarbons. Each has to be dealt with.

38
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:55,483
A solvent absorbs the acid gases and is regenerated for reuse. Dehydration removes the water. Hydrogen sulfide is converted into solid sulfur.

39
00:06:55,983 --> 00:07:07,694
Cooling then recovers the natural gas liquids, which are split into ethane, propane, butanes and natural gasoline. What remains is methane-rich sales gas.

40
00:07:09,594 --> 00:07:17,859
Sales gas moves through transmission pipelines. Compressor stations raise the pressure again and again along the route.

41
00:07:18,359 --> 00:07:30,818
The pipeline itself holds gas, called linepack. And underground storage, in depleted reservoirs, aquifers or salt caverns, balances supply against seasonal demand.

42
00:07:32,718 --> 00:07:45,857
Where a pipeline cannot reach, gas is liquefied. Cooled to about −162 °C, it becomes a liquid that takes up roughly 600 times less space.

43
00:07:46,357 --> 00:07:57,417
This is a physical change, not a chemical one: LNG is still mostly methane. It is not the same as LPG, which is mainly propane and butane.

44
00:07:57,917 --> 00:08:06,858
Specialised ships carry it in insulated tanks. Heat leaking in creates boil-off gas, which is recovered or used as fuel.

45
00:08:07,358 --> 00:08:14,297
At the import terminal, vaporisers warm the LNG back into gas for the pipeline grid.

46
00:08:15,997 --> 00:08:23,099
Part three: downstream. Turn crude oil into products, and deliver them to the people who use them.

47
00:08:24,999 --> 00:08:36,080
Downstream begins at the refinery. Crude oils differ in density, sulfur and boiling range, so each grade is sampled, stored separately and scheduled.

48
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:47,965
First the crude is preheated with recovered heat and desalted: wash water picks up the salts and is separated out. Then a furnace brings it to distillation temperature.

49
00:08:49,865 --> 00:09:00,143
In the distillation column, hot vapour rises and liquid falls across trays. Light components travel to the top; heavy ones leave lower down.

50
00:09:00,643 --> 00:09:12,778
Side draws take off naphtha, kerosene and gas oils. Distillation separates molecules; it does not break them. And these are intermediate streams, not finished fuels.

51
00:09:13,278 --> 00:09:22,250
The heavy residue goes to a second column under vacuum, where low pressure lets it boil at a lower temperature, without cracking.

52
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:35,218
Conversion units change the molecules themselves. A fluid catalytic cracker uses hot, circulating catalyst to break heavy molecules into lighter ones.

53
00:09:35,718 --> 00:09:43,076
Coke left on the catalyst is burned off in a regenerator, which reheats the catalyst for another pass.

54
00:09:43,576 --> 00:09:54,439
Hydrocracking does a similar job with hydrogen and a catalyst. Coking turns the heaviest residue into lighter streams and solid petroleum coke.

55
00:09:56,339 --> 00:10:07,225
Other units improve quality rather than size. Hydrotreating uses hydrogen and a catalyst to strip out sulfur, which leaves as hydrogen sulfide.

56
00:10:07,725 --> 00:10:19,844
Reforming rearranges naphtha into high-octane components, and makes hydrogen as a by-product. Isomerisation and alkylation build better gasoline components.

57
00:10:20,344 --> 00:10:27,675
Three different jobs: remove contaminants, rearrange molecules, and crack large molecules.

58
00:10:29,575 --> 00:10:40,623
Finished fuels are blended from several components to hit many specifications at once: octane, sulfur, volatility, density and flash point.

59
00:10:41,123 --> 00:10:51,056
Laboratory tests and online analysers check every batch. Only a certified tank is released. Off-spec product is held and corrected.

60
00:10:52,956 --> 00:11:04,690
Behind the process units run the utilities: steam, power, cooling water, fuel gas and hydrogen, along with wastewater treatment and sulfur recovery.

61
00:11:05,190 --> 00:11:12,116
The flare is a safety system for upsets and relief. It is not the routine destination for gas.

62
00:11:12,616 --> 00:11:25,589
And not every molecule becomes fuel. Ethane, LPG and naphtha feed steam crackers that make ethylene and propylene: the building blocks of plastics, fibres and packaging.

63
00:11:26,089 --> 00:11:32,056
Refineries also yield lubricants, waxes, bitumen and petroleum coke.

64
00:11:33,956 --> 00:11:44,060
Finished products fan out by pipeline, ship, rail and truck to terminals. Road fuels reach service stations, and jet fuel reaches airports.

65
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:53,920
Gas reaches power plants and industry, or passes through pressure reduction into city networks, where an odorant is added for safety.

66
00:11:55,820 --> 00:12:08,103
Across the whole chain, every stage balances what comes in against products, inventories, internal use and losses. Volumes can change. Mass is conserved.

67
00:12:08,603 --> 00:12:18,470
Information also flows backwards. Market demand shapes refinery plans, and pipeline limits can hold back production at the wells.

68
00:12:18,970 --> 00:12:27,958
And when a field reaches the end of its life, the wells are permanently plugged, the equipment is removed, and the site is restored.

69
00:12:29,858 --> 00:12:42,112
Upstream discovers and produces hydrocarbons. Midstream connects production with processing facilities and markets. Downstream transforms and delivers useful products.

70
00:12:42,612 --> 00:12:54,417
Together they form one interconnected system of wells, facilities, transport networks, measurements and decisions, linking the reservoir to the final consumer.
